WEBVTT

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:13.500
Right, so today we're picking up where we left off, and we're looking at how the resting membrane potential is actually established across the axolemma.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:24.000
The key player here is the sodium-potassium ATPase. It pumps three sodium ions out of the cell for every two potassium ions it brings in, and that stoichiometry matters.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:35.200
Because it's electrogenic, it contributes directly to the negative interior. But the bigger contribution is the differential permeability — at rest the membrane is far more permeable to potassium than to sodium.

00:34:18.100 --> 00:34:28.900
Now, this next part is examinable, so do write it down. Myelination increases conduction velocity, and the mechanism is saltatory conduction.

00:34:28.900 --> 00:34:41.000
The action potential regenerates only at the nodes of Ranvier. Between the nodes the signal spreads passively, which is much faster than continuous propagation along an unmyelinated fibre.

00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:52.300
So when you're comparing a myelinated A-alpha fibre with an unmyelinated C fibre, the difference in velocity is roughly two orders of magnitude, and you should be able to say why.
